.TH FIXPERMS 1 "October 2026" "fixperms 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fixperms \- repair owner, mode and ACL of files created from an elevated Cygwin shell

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fixperms
[\-n] [\-v] [\-d] [\-u USER] [\-g GROUP] [\-w] [\-x] [\-f]
.I path
\&...

.SH DESCRIPTION
Files and directories created from an elevated ("Run as administrator") Cygwin shell \- a
.B pip install
into
.IR /usr/local ,
a build in a project directory, anything under
.I ~/.local
\- often come out owned by the Administrators group, with Windows ACLs that a normal, non-elevated
session cannot read or write: "Permission denied" on
.BR ls ,
failed imports, and a trailing
.B +
in
.BR "ls \-l" .
.B chmod
alone does not fix them, because the trouble is in ACL entries it leaves in place: named user and group
entries (such as
.B user:chris:\-\-\-
or
.BR group:Administrators:rwx ),
a mask, NULL SID and deny ACEs, and default (inheritable) entries such as
.B default:user::\-\-\-
that hand the same damage on to every new file.

.B fixperms
walks each
.I path
top-down, without following symbolic links, and for every file and directory:
.IP 1. 4
sets the owner and group to the invoking user (or
.BR \-\-user / \-\-group )
with
.BR chown ;
.IP 2. 4
replaces the whole ACL with a minimal one using
.BR "setfacl \-s" :
directories get
.BR u::rwx,g::r\-x,o::r\-x ,
plus the same as default entries so that new files and subdirectories inherit a clean ACL;
files get
.BR u::rw\-,g::r\-\-,o::r\-\- ,
or
.B rwxr\-xr\-x
if they are executable (see below). Cygwin turns this into a short, canonical Windows ACL: the owner,
the group and Everyone, plus CREATOR OWNER, CREATOR GROUP and Everyone entries that are inherited by new
files. Setuid, setgid and sticky bits are kept;
.IP 3. 4
if
.B setfacl
cannot set the ACL (a file system Cygwin cannot map POSIX ACLs onto, for example), resets the Windows ACL
to the inherited one with
.B "icacls /reset"
and then sets the mode with
.BR chmod .
.PP
Entries whose owner, group and ACL are already right are left alone, so a second run changes nothing.
Directories are fixed before they are listed, so a directory the user cannot even open is fixed first and
then descended into.

.SS Executable files
A file is made executable for everyone if any of its execute bits was set, or if it starts with
.B #!
(a script),
.B MZ
(a Windows .exe, .dll or Python .pyd, which Cygwin will not load without execute permission) or
.B \e177ELF
\- an "a+rX"-style rule, never a blanket
.BR +x .
With
.BR \-\-exec\-by\-content ,
existing execute bits are ignored and only the content decides; use it when a tree's execute bits cannot be
trusted, as with files created under Windows-default ACLs, which often show
.B rwx
on everything. A file that cannot be read until it is fixed is checked after its ACL has been set.

.SS Ownership and elevation
Changing a file's owner to yourself from a non-elevated shell works only if you already own it or the ACL
grants you "take ownership"; files owned by Administrators usually need an elevated shell. When a
.B chown
fails like that,
.B fixperms
says so, carries on with the rest, and at the end prints the command to run again from an elevated Cygwin
terminal. It never touches the ACL of a file whose owner it could not change.
.B fixperms
counts a shell as elevated when
.B "id \-G"
includes the Administrators group (544), which Cygwin lists only for an elevated token.

.SS Safety
.B fixperms
refuses, without
.BR \-\-force ,
to work on
.IR / ,
the Cygwin root (as
.IR / ,
.I D:/cygwin
or
.IR /cygdrive/d/cygwin ),
a drive root, or a top-level directory such as
.I /usr
or
.IR C:/Users .
It never follows a symbolic link (a link's own owner is changed with
.BR "chown \-h" ,
nothing else), and does not descend into a directory on another file system. Paths may contain spaces and
any other characters.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be changed, and predict which changes would need an elevated shell; change nothing.
Implies
.BR \-v .
A directory that cannot be read until it is fixed is reported as not looked into.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
List every change, with the old and new owner, group and mode. Give it twice to list unchanged entries too.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-no\-recurse
Fix only the named paths, not what is under them.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-user USER
Owner to set. Default: the invoking user.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-group GROUP
Group to set, by name or number. Default: the primary group of the invoking user (or of
.BR \-\-user ).
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-group\-write
Give the group write access: 775 and 664 instead of 755 and 644. Without it, write access for the group
and for others is removed.
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-exec\-by\-content
Ignore existing execute bits; set them only on files that start with
.BR #! ,
.B MZ
or ELF magic.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Allow
.IR / ,
the Cygwin root, drive roots and top-level directories.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the version and exit.

.SH OUTPUT
Each change (with
.B \-v
or
.BR \-n )
is one line; a
.B +
after the old mode means the old ACL had extended entries that were removed:
.RS
.nf
fix site-packages/pyte: mode dr-xrwxr-x+ -> 755 (ACL cleaned)
fix site-packages/pyte/__init__.py: owner Administrators -> chris; mode -r--rw-r--+ -> 644 (ACL cleaned)
.fi
.RE
Then a summary, and on standard error each path that could not be fixed, with the reason:
.RS
.nf
fixperms: 412 checked, 3 already fine, 409 fixed (409 owner/group, 409 mode/ACL), 2 symlinks not followed
.fi
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
See what an elevated pip install did, then fix it from an elevated Cygwin terminal:
.RS
.nf
.B fixperms \-n /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages
.B fixperms \-v /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages
.fi
.RE

Fix a project directory, leaving it group-writable:
.RS
.B fixperms \-w ~/github/myproject
.RE

Fix just one directory entry, not its contents:
.RS
.B fixperms \-d /usr/local/bin
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything is (or, with
.BR \-n ,
would be) fixed.
.TP
.B 1
Something could not be fixed (or would need an elevated shell), a path does not exist, or a path was
refused.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or a required tool
.RB ( stat ,
.BR chown ,
.BR chmod ,
.BR getfacl ,
.BR setfacl ,
.BR realpath )
is missing.

.SH NOTES
Written for Cygwin, where
.B getfacl
and
.B setfacl
map POSIX ACLs onto Windows ACLs; it also works on Linux file systems with ACL support, where "elevated"
means root.

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chown (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR setfacl (1),
.BR icacls ,
the Cygwin User's Guide section "POSIX accounts, permission, and security"
.RI ( https://cygwin.com/cygwin\-ug\-net/ntsec.html )
